# Write SvelteKit apps

Declare the browser app participant in the app's native `.trellis` source and generate it before checking or building the app.

```ts
import { createTrellisApp } from "@qlever-llc/trellis-svelte";
import { participant } from "../../.trellis/ts/participants/orders-app/mod.ts";

export const trellisApp = createTrellisApp({
  participant,
  trellisUrl: "https://trellis.example.com",
});
```

Use the generated participant for browser connection and bind flows. Keep secrets and durable server credentials in server-only modules; browser trust state uses the Trellis browser storage adapter.

## Declare app authority

Add the APIs the app consumes to `trellis.toml`, resolve them with
`trellis update`, and select the required actions in native IDL `use required`
blocks. Use `use optional` only for authority the user can decline. Run
`trellis generate` after changes, then run the app's normal check and build.
Do not author `defineAppContract` objects or generate participant evidence in
browser code.

## Own one browser connection

Mount `TrellisProvider` with the app owner near the root layout. In child
components, call `trellisApp.getTrellis()` during component initialization to
obtain the typed generated caller. `trellisApp.getConnection()` returns a
Svelte-reactive connection; read its `status` in markup or a derived expression.
Show connecting, disconnected, and failed states rather than silently rendering
an empty data view.

The app owner can live in a module, but connected clients belong to Svelte
context. Never put a user's connected client in module-global mutable state on
the server. Keep server-only helpers under `$lib/server` or in `.server.ts`
files. Do not disable SSR globally merely to hide an incorrect browser import.

## Call the generated API

Generated callers expose flat selected methods, for example `ordersGet(...)`,
not a hand-constructed RPC namespace. Handle their `Result` values explicitly.
Use operations for asynchronous workflows, generated event subscriptions for
live delivery, and the returned cancellation handles when leaving a view. Do
not subscribe through raw NATS or recreate signed wire envelopes.

Let the provider own browser authentication redirects and the returned callback.
An expired plain callback starts a new flow; explicit approval denial remains a
user-visible outcome. The browser's durable trust floor survives logout, so do
not clear IndexedDB as a routine recovery technique.

## Verify a browser consumer

Keep Vite aliases, npm/JSR imports, and TypeScript path mappings aligned with the
generated module's imports. Run the app's normal `svelte-check` and production
build, then exercise login, a generated call, disconnect/reconnect, and logout
against a real runtime. See [the Svelte AI guide](/llms-svelte.txt) for resolver
details and [/api](/api) for current package APIs.
